y shutoffs. ### Think of it like your kitchen stove When you light a burner, the flame stays at the burner head. You don’t worry about it traveling up the gas hose and blowing up your house or the gas main under the street. The exact same physics applies to a house fire — the point of combustion is the point where gas meets air, and it never moves upstream. I get that it feels unintuitive because we imagine a lit fuse leading to a powder keg. But a gas line isn’t a fuse — it’s a fuel line in an oxygen-free environment. I’m happy to go deeper on any part of this if you’d like, but I promise, this is one of those cases where reality really is opposite to the Hollywood image.
