he fire hasn’t “propagated backwards”; it’s just a new burn point further along the physical pipe, but still only where gas meets air. So the pipe could fail progressively backward along its length if the fire is progressively heating it, but that’s just sequential ruptures, each producing a separate external flame, not a single detonation wave racing back to the city main. And this only happens for the above-ground portion. Once the pipe enters the ground, soil acts as a heat sink and prevents this progressive failure. Buried lines don’t get hot enough to rupture from a house fire. ### The “torch” comparison is actually more instructive than the stove Let’s upgrade the analogy: consider an industrial gas cutting torch. The flame at the tip is far hotter (around 3,000°C) than a structure fire (typically 500–1,100°C). Yet the flame never travels back into the hose. Why? Because the hoses carry pure fuel and pure oxygen, separated. Only at the tip do they mix. In a natural gas line, you don’t even have the oxygen hose — it’s all fuel. So even a 3,000° flame held against the pipe won’t cause a flashback, because there’s never a combustible mixture inside the pipe to flash back through. ### What about detonation of pre-heated gas? This is where some people get tripped up. Could the heat from a structure fire cause the gas inside the pipe to become so hot that it auto-ignites *if* oxygen suddenly entered? But again, oxygen cannot enter because the pipe is pressurized — gas is pushing out. Even if the pipe ruptured and air rushed in, you’d only get a momentary mixing zone right at the rupture, which would ignite locally, not travel back. To get a traveling detonation, you’d need a long column of well-mixed fuel-air at the right concentration throughout the pipe. That’s physically impossible in a positive-pressure gas line. ### Real-world tests and standards Gas utilities and fire researchers have studied exactly this scenario. The National Fire Protection Association (NFP