t’s simple but impossible vs. story that’s complex but physically allowed.” When you frame it that way, the complexity becomes a necessity, not a preference. ### Why the complex explanation isn’t actually that complex if you look at it piece by piece It might help to break down what I’m describing into a handful of straightforward, observable events that chain together: 1. **Pipe gets very hot from the fire.** (Happens all the time; metal conducts heat.) 2. **Hot pipe softens and ruptures under gas pressure.** (Again, mundane. Happens in fires constantly.) 3. **Gas shoots out the new hole and catches fire.** (Gas + air + heat = fire, exactly what you’d expect.) 4. **That new flame is now heating the next section of pipe, which might also have been weakened by the fire.** (A straightforward chain of heating.) 5. **Repeat 2–4 a couple of times.** (Now you have multiple flame sources along the pipe’s path, looking like the fire “jumped.”) That’s it. No internal detonation. No flashback. Each step is something both of us can agree happens in fires constantly. The only thing that makes it seem “complicated” is that I had to explain why it’s *not* the internal version, which required bringing in oxygen, fluid dynamics, and so on. But the actual sequence of events is just a chain of mundane ruptures and ignitions. ### The hidden simplicity behind my argument In fact, you could argue my underlying point is even simpler than the “fire traveled back” idea. It boils down to one sentence: **You can’t have a fire where there’s no air.** Everything else — the sequential ruptures, the migration — is just the consequence of that one simple rule. - “Fire traveled back through the pipe” requires you to think, “In this one special case, fire doesn’t need air.” - “The pipe broke in multiple places and gas lit on fire outside each break” requires only, “Fire needs air,” which is true everywhere else in the universe. So which is simpler: a universal rule with a messy but real-world sequence of events, or a story that requires the universe to make an exception when a house is burning? ### Why we naturally prefer the simple wrong version over the complex right one There’s a famous principle in psychology: the **availability heuristic**. Events that are easy to imagine seem more probable. “Flame shooting backwards through a tube” is easy to visualize (we’ve all seen fuses burn, gas torches, explosive trails in movies). “Heated pipe failing sequentially while underground gas migrates through soil” is much harder to visualize, so it feels less likely, even though it’s what physically occurs. You’re not alone. Firefighters themselves used to believe the simple version for the exact same reason. It took decades of accident investigations and experiments showing zero internal flame travel to shift the expert consensus. So I’m not asking you to be gullible — I’m asking you to make the same shift experts made when they realized the simple story couldn’t beat the oxygen problem. ### The bottom line If someone tells you, “I saw water flowing uphill,” you wouldn’t need a complicated explanation to doubt them — you’d know gravity makes that impossible. The fire triangle (fuel + oxygen + heat) is just as fundamental to chemistry as gravity is to physics. When a witness says, “I saw fire travel inside a gas line,” they’re essentially saying, “I saw fire burn without oxygen.” I can’t accept that at face value, not because I trust reports and engineers more than people, but because I trust the fire triangle more than any human narrative. The complicated explanation is what you get when you take the witness’s honest observations and the fire triangle’s non-negotiable requirements, and you try to make them both true at the same time. That’s all I’m doing. Not dismissing anyone, not choosing complexity for its own sake. Just refusing to believe that a fundamental law of nature took the day off during that fire. Does that make sense why the “complicated” path is actually the only one left standing?
